Fingerprint:
```dart
  static Future<String> fingerprint() async {
    final tables = Map<String, Object>.from(await AppDb.I.exportTables())..remove('exportedAt');
    final payload = StringBuffer(_canonical(tables));
    final files = <String>[]; // entries
    final dir = Directory(ImageStore.root);
    if (await dir.exists()) {
      await for (final e in dir.list(recursive: true)) {
        if (e is File) {
          final st = await e.stat();
          files.add('${p.relative(e.path, from: ImageStore.root)}|${st.size}|${st.modified.millisecondsSinceEpoch}');
        }
      }
    }
    files.sort();
    payload.write('\n#images\n'); payload.write(files.join('\n'));
    return sha256.convert(utf8.encode(payload.toString())).toString();
  }
```
Canonical: sort map keys recursively; lists: for tables, sort list entries by canonical string? If two exports order differ but same data, fingerprint differs. To be robust, sort list of maps by canonical string. But careful: list order might be meaningful (e.g., clothes_ids is a JSON string field, not list). Tables rows order not meaningful. Sort all lists? Some values may be lists (e.g. none? export rows values are primitives/strings). canonical can sort lists of maps only. Simpler: implement canonicalize with maps sorted; for lists, if all elements are Map, sort by canonical; else keep order.

Write:
```dart
  static String _canonical(Object? v) {
    if (v is Map) {
      final keys = v.keys.map((e) => e.toString()).toList()..sort();
      return '{${keys.map((k) => '${jsonEncode(k)}:${_canonical(v[k])}').join(',')}}';
    }
    if (v is List) {
      final parts = v.map(_canonical).toList();
      if (v.isNotEmpty && v.every((e) => e is Map)) parts.sort();
      return '[${parts.join(',')}]';
    }
    return jsonEncode(v);
  }
```

